1
00:00:00,426 --> 00:00:01,872
9:04 in the morning.

2
00:00:01,872 --> 00:00:06,353
An exam paper is already on WhatsApp,
an hour before the exam.

3
00:00:06,353 --> 00:00:08,088
500 colleges printed it.

4
00:00:08,088 --> 00:00:09,534
Which one leaked it?

5
00:00:11,363 --> 00:00:15,612
Leak Trace gives every college the same
questions and the same marks.

6
00:00:15,612 --> 00:00:17,830
But each copy hides its own pattern:

7
00:00:17,830 --> 00:00:21,094
its own question order, and its
own approved wording.

8
00:00:22,624 --> 00:00:23,908
There are three layers:

9
00:00:23,908 --> 00:00:27,202
the order, the wording, and the
numbers and answer letters.

10
00:00:27,202 --> 00:00:31,557
A leaker can't change that last layer without
making the leaked answers wrong.

11
00:00:33,181 --> 00:00:34,411
Let's be the leaker.

12
00:00:34,411 --> 00:00:38,283
We retype seven questions, with typos,
and no question numbers.

13
00:00:38,688 --> 00:00:43,069
Leak Trace names a college only when
the odds reach 10,000 to 1.

14
00:00:43,069 --> 00:00:44,027
Here, it does.

15
00:00:45,539 --> 00:00:48,993
Each line adds evidence, until one
college crosses the bar.

16
00:00:51,404 --> 00:00:52,559
Now try to dodge it.

17
00:00:52,559 --> 00:00:54,926
Reword every line, and shuffle the order.

18
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:59,241
With the order and wording gone, it
holds back instead of guessing.

19
00:00:59,241 --> 00:01:00,933
No innocent college is named.

20
00:01:02,765 --> 00:01:05,757
Even a leaked answer key gives
the college away.

21
00:01:05,757 --> 00:01:10,121
With coded keys, any two colleges differ
in at least 12 of 20 answers.

22
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:15,102
16 answers, one in ten of them wrong, and
the source is still named.

23
00:01:20,324 --> 00:01:22,369
Now, 200 random leaks at once.

24
00:01:23,292 --> 00:01:25,245
Innocent colleges blamed:

25
00:01:25,245 --> 00:01:25,636
zero.

26
00:01:25,636 --> 00:01:28,604
Across 148,000 attack leaks, just one,

27
00:01:28,604 --> 00:01:32,823
from a chance match on a copy from
outside the system.

28
00:01:34,648 --> 00:01:36,119
And there is a proof.

29
00:01:36,119 --> 00:01:41,583
Under our model, a copy from outside the
system clears the bar at most once in

30
00:01:41,583 --> 00:01:42,494
10,000 times.

31
00:01:42,494 --> 00:01:45,437
We checked it on 800,000 simulated copies.

32
00:01:47,457 --> 00:01:52,064
It is all in a 15-page research paper,
with open code and data.

33
00:01:52,064 --> 00:01:54,478
Every result so far is simulated.

34
00:01:54,478 --> 00:01:56,964
Next comes a pilot in one college.

35
00:01:58,185 --> 00:01:58,815
Leak Trace.

36
00:01:58,815 --> 00:02:02,197
Tracing a leaked exam paper to the
college that printed it.

